     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "Lencho is presented as an 'ox of a man', a poor but industrious farmer who works like an animal in his fields and knows that his survival depends on his labour and the mercy of the sky. He is optimistic by nature: when clouds gather in the north-east he happily predicts rain, and he counts the first drops like new coins, dreaming of the harvest they promise. Disaster, however, tests him. After the hour-long hailstorm destroys everything, his soul fills with sadness, yet he does not collapse; instead his deep, childlike faith rises, and he writes a letter to God with the confidence of a son writing to a father, certain that God sees everything, even what lies in one's conscience. His faith is total but naive, for he never wonders how a letter can reach God without an address, or how money can come down from heaven by post. When seventy pesos arrive, he is not surprised, because his confidence in God never wavers; instead he becomes angry at the missing thirty and accuses the post office employees of being a bunch of crooks. Here lies his tragic flaw: his faith in God is blind, but his faith in humanity is absent, and he repays secret charity with insult. Lencho thus remains a memorable mixture of hard work, hope, innocence and ingratitude, a simple man whose noble faith and unjust suspicion together create the story's unforgettable irony."
