     "answer": "The postmaster of the small village post office is a fat, amiable fellow, cheerful by nature, who first appears laughing heartily at the absurd letter addressed to God. That laughter, however, is only the surface of his character. When he reads the letter properly, the depth of the farmer's faith moves him, and he says that he wishes he had such faith himself. From amusement he passes to action: he decides to reply so that the writer's faith in God may not be shaken, and this decision reveals his compassion and respect for another person's belief. He cannot gather the full hundred pesos, but he gives part of his own salary and persuades his employees and friends to contribute for an act of charity, finally sending seventy pesos in an envelope signed with the single word 'God'. He does all this anonymously, seeking neither credit nor thanks, and he receives in return the bitter accusation that his staff are a bunch of crooks; yet the story tells of no complaint from him. The postmaster therefore stands for quiet, practical goodness: he cannot perform miracles, but he becomes the miracle for a stranger. His character teaches that kindness does not need applause, that faith in humanity can protect faith in God, and that a little generosity, given without show, can keep hope alive in a ruined household."
