    {
     "question": "When and where was Nelson Mandela sworn in as the first black President of South Africa?",
     "marks": 2,
     "type": "very-short",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "Nelson Mandela was sworn in as the first black President of South Africa on 10 May 1994, in the amphitheatre of the Union Buildings in Pretoria, before the largest gathering of international leaders ever on South African soil. His daughter Zenani accompanied him, and he pledged to obey and uphold the Constitution of the new democracy."
    },
    {
     "question": "What was the policy of apartheid? What deep wound did it create?",
     "marks": 3,
     "type": "short",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "Apartheid was the official policy of racial segregation practised in South Africa, under which people were divided and discriminated against on the basis of the colour of their skin, and the white minority ruled over the black majority. Mandela describes it as a system that created a deep and lasting wound in his country and his people. It robbed the black citizens of their land, their votes, their dignity and their basic honourable freedoms such as earning a living and living with family, and it left scars of poverty and humiliation that the new nation of 1994 had to heal."
    },
    {
     "question": "Why were two national anthems sung on the day of the inauguration? What does this tell us about the new nation?",
     "marks": 3,
     "type": "short",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "On inauguration day two national anthems were sung: Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika, the anthem of the black people, and Die Stem, the old anthem of the whites. Singing both was a deliberate act of reconciliation, showing that the new South Africa belonged equally to blacks and whites and that the wounds of the past would be healed, not avenged. Mandela himself honoured the military generals who would once have arrested him and shared the ceremony with leaders of the old regime. The twin anthems, like the twin obligations and the two colours of the new flag, announced a nation born of unity, forgiveness and shared hope."
    },
    {
     "question": "How does Nelson Mandela define courage? Why does he call it the triumph over fear rather than the absence of fear?",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "Mandela's definition of courage is one of the finest lessons of the chapter. He says that courage is not the absence of fear but the triumph over it, and explains that the brave man is not he who does not feel afraid, but he who conquers fear. He supports this with honest self-knowledge: he confesses that he was not born with a hunger to be free, that he had his share of moments of fear, and that he learned to hide his fear behind a mask of bravery so that others might feel stronger. Courage, therefore, is not a gift of birth but a habit of the heart, practised again and again. Mandela learned it from the people of South Africa, whose fearless endurance of apartheid he says he can never repay, and from comrades like Oliver Tambo and Walter Sisulu, who stood firm when resistance meant prison or death. The definition matters because it makes courage democratic: any ordinary man or woman can be brave, since bravery does not require the absence of fear, only its mastery. It also explains Mandela's own life: nearly thirty years in prison did not produce a broken man but a leader whose fear had been conquered and converted into strength, forgiveness and the will to free not only the oppressed but also the oppressor who imprisoned them."
    },
    {
     "question": "What are the twin obligations that every man has, according to Mandela? Why was it impossible to fulfil both under apartheid?",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "Mandela believes that in every country and in every life there come moments when a man must choose between two sets of duties, and that every man has twin obligations: the first to his family, to his parents, his wife and his children, and the second to his community, his people and his country. In a free and civil society a man is able to fulfil both obligations and receive credit for his work, raising his family with dignity while serving society openly. But in a country like South Africa under apartheid it was almost impossible for a man of Mandela's birth and colour to honour both. A person who tried to fulfil his duty to his people was automatically punished: he was hounded, isolated from his family, banned from work and, like Mandela himself, thrown into prison for decades. Choosing the family meant betraying the people, and choosing the people meant abandoning the family, so the system broke both duties at once. Mandela chose his people, losing personal freedom for the sake of collective freedom, and this sacrifice makes his reflection deeply moving. The twin obligations also teach students that citizenship is not only private happiness but public responsibility, and that a just society is one in which no one is ever forced to choose between home and country."
    },
    {
     "question": "'The oppressor and the oppressed alike are robbed of their humanity.' Explain this statement of Mandela with reference to the chapter.",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "This statement contains Mandela's deepest insight into the nature of injustice. It is natural to think that in an unequal system only the victim suffers, but Mandela insists that the oppressor must be liberated just as surely as the oppressed. The oppressor is robbed of his humanity because a man who takes away another man's freedom becomes a prisoner of hatred; he is locked behind the bars of prejudice and narrow-mindedness, and he is not truly free while he fears the very people he oppresses. Every law that humiliates another also coarsens the law-maker; every chain placed on a fellow human being forges a chain around the jailer's own soul. The oppressed, meanwhile, is robbed of humanity through indignity, hunger, separation from family and the daily insult of discrimination, which makes a man feel less than a man. Mandela's genius was to see that apartheid deformed both communities, and that a free South Africa therefore had to liberate both. That is why he walked out of prison preaching reconciliation, saluted the generals of the old regime and invited his former enemies into the government. The statement also carries a universal lesson: freedom is not merely casting off one's own chains but living in a way that respects and enhances the freedom of others, for no one is free while anyone is unfree."
    },
    {
     "question": "Read the extract and answer the questions: No one is born hating another person because of the colour of his skin, or his background, or his religion. People must learn to hate, and if they can learn to hate, they can be taught to love, for love comes more naturally to the human heart than its opposite. (a) What is the source of hatred according to Mandela? (b) Why is love more natural than hate? (c) What hope does this idea give to a divided society?",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "extract-based",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "1) (a) According to Mandela, hatred is not inborn; it is learned from the environment, from education, prejudice and the example of others, and is directed at people because of their colour, background or religion. 2) (b) Love is more natural than hate because it comes more readily to the human heart; Mandela calls man's goodness a flame that can be hidden but never extinguished, so love is the heart's original language and hate only an acquired distortion. 3) (c) This idea gives a divided society the hope that change is possible: since hate had to be taught, it can be untaught, and the very people trained in prejudice can be re-educated in love. 4) Mandela himself proved this by refusing to let bitterness rule his life after nearly thirty years in prison, and by building a rainbow nation with his former enemies. 5) The passage also puts responsibility on educators and parents, for what is taught can be changed by better teaching."
    },
    {
     "question": "What lessons can students and citizens of today learn from Nelson Mandela's 'Long Walk to Freedom'?",
     "marks": 4,
     "type": "value-based",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "Mandela's chapter offers lessons that go far beyond one nation's history. First, courage is a habit, not a birthright: we should treat fear as something to be conquered, since the brave man is he who conquers fear. Second, freedom is collective: no one is truly free while his neighbours are unfree, so we must respect and enhance the freedom of others in school, at work and in society. Third, hate is learned and love is natural: we can refuse the lessons of prejudice passed on by our surroundings, because love comes more naturally to the human heart. Fourth, forgiveness is strength, not weakness: Mandela honoured the generals who once would have arrested him and shared power with his old enemies, proving that reconciliation builds nations where revenge destroys them. Finally, duty has two faces: we owe care to our family and service to our community, and a good citizen tries to honour both. A young reader who carries these five lessons forward walks, in a small way, Mandela's long walk to freedom."
    }
