    {
     "question": "Why was the young seagull afraid to fly? How did his family react to his fear?",
     "marks": 3,
     "type": "short",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "The young seagull was afraid because he felt certain that his wings would never support him; the great expanse of sea stretched down miles below his ledge, and the drop terrified him though his wings were as long as his family's. His parents had taught his two brothers and his sister to skim the waves and dive for fish, but he remained alone on the ledge, running back to his little hole under it. His family reacted harshly: his father and mother came around calling to him shrilly, upbraiding him and threatening to let him starve on his ledge unless he flew away, while his brothers and sister enjoyed their food in his sight. Yet no taunt could move him, for his fear was stronger than his hunger."
    },
    {
     "question": "How did the mother seagull tempt the young one to make his first flight? What does her trick teach us?",
     "marks": 3,
     "type": "short",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "The mother seagull understood that scolding had failed, so she used temptation. She took a piece of fish and flew across to him with it, coming almost within the reach of his beak; the hungry young bird, surprised and joyful, dived at the fish with a loud scream. At the crucial moment she flew upward and halted just out of reach, so that his dive carried him outward into space. As he fell, his wings spread outwards, the wind rushed against them, and he was flying. Her trick teaches that encouragement and clever challenge work better than threats, and that courage often awakens only when necessity forces a person to act."
    },
    {
     "question": "Describe the young seagull's first flight in your own words. How did his feelings change during it?",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "very-high",
     "answer": "The young seagull's first flight begins in hunger and ends in joy. Starving for twenty-four hours while his family flew and fed, he saw his mother approaching with a piece of fish and could not resist diving at it. The moment she halted just out of reach, his own momentum carried him off the ledge, and he fell outward and downward into space, terrified, his wings refusing to obey. Then instinct took over: his wings spread outwards, the wind rushed against his breast feathers and under his stomach, and the next moment he was soaring upwards instead of falling. His terror dissolved into delight; he called happily to his mother, and his brothers and sister came flying around him, curveting, banking, soaring and diving in celebration. He flew out over the green sea he had feared so much, swooped low, and finally landed on its surface, where his legs sank into the water and, when his belly touched it, he was momentarily too frightened to fly; but he floated safely, for his body was made for the sea as well as the sky. The journey of his feelings runs from doubt to panic, from panic to instinctive action, and from action to the proud joy of mastery, teaching that the first step, once taken, turns fear into freedom."
    },
    {
     "question": "'Fear has to be faced to be conquered.' How does 'His First Flight' prove this statement?",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "O'Flaherty's story is a perfect illustration of the truth that fear dissolves only when it is faced. The young seagull's wings were ready; they were as long as those of his brothers and sister, yet he stood starving on his ledge because he was certain they would never support him. His fear fed on itself: the longer he looked down at the miles of empty air, the stronger his conviction of helplessness grew, and no amount of parental scolding could cure it. The cure came only through action. When hunger and his mother's tempting fish forced him to dive, he left the ledge, and at the very instant of danger his wings spread outwards and did their work. The fear that had ruled him for a whole day vanished in a single second of flight, and he was soon soaring, diving and floating on the green sea with the joy of a free bird. The story proves that fear is largely an illusion of inaction: the drop that seemed fatal became the very path to safety once it was entered. For students, the message is practical: hesitate before an examination, a speech or a new task, and the fear grows; take the first step, and the wings of ability, always present, begin to work."
    },
    {
     "question": "Who do you think helped the narrator of 'The Black Aeroplane'? Was it a mystery or a miracle? Justify your answer.",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "long",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "The rescue in 'The Black Aeroplane' is left deliberately unexplained, and both a mystery and a miracle can be argued. The mystery reading is rational: a pilot alone in the dark, panicking, with failed instruments, may have imagined the black aeroplane, and the simple act of following what he believed was another plane kept him flying steadily, lowering him until he broke out of the cloud and found the runway himself. The miracle reading is spiritual: help appeared exactly when fuel and hope were nearly exhausted, guided him without a single spoken word and vanished the moment safety was reached, while the radar denied that any other aircraft existed in the sky that night. Many readers therefore see the stranger as a guardian angel or an unknown benefactor whose identity the pilot, and we, can never know. What is certain is the moral either way: in moments of total helplessness a person must keep acting calmly, follow guidance when it comes and trust that help exists even when it cannot be explained. The narrator's obedience, like an obedient child, saved his life; his gratitude, searching the empty sky for his rescuer, reminds us to be thankful for unseen hands that carry us through our own storms."
    },
    {
     "question": "Read the extract and answer the questions: Inside the clouds, everything was suddenly black. It was impossible to see anything outside the aeroplane. The old aeroplane jumped and twisted in the air. Then the compass and the other instruments stopped working. (a) Why did the narrator enter the clouds although they were dangerous? (b) What problems did he face inside them? (c) How does the writer create suspense here?",
     "marks": 5,
     "type": "extract-based",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "1) (a) The narrator entered the clouds because he was flying home to England and dreamed of a big breakfast with his family; although Paris control had told him to turn twelve degrees west, he decided to go through the huge storm clouds instead of returning to Paris. 2) (b) Inside the clouds he faced total darkness, for nothing could be seen outside the aeroplane windows; the old plane jumped and twisted in the air; then the compass and all other instruments stopped working, the radio went dead, and he had fuel for only five or ten minutes more. 3) (c) The writer creates suspense by stripping the pilot of every sense and support one by one: first sight, then direction, then communication, then fuel, so that each sentence deepens the reader's feeling of helplessness. 4) The blackness and the twisting motion make the danger physical, while the exact minutes of fuel place a ticking clock in the scene. 5) This suspense prepares the reader for the wonder of the mysterious black aeroplane that suddenly appears to help."
    },
    {
     "question": "What values do 'His First Flight' and 'The Black Aeroplane' teach us for overcoming difficulties in life?",
     "marks": 4,
     "type": "value-based",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "Together the two stories teach a compact set of life values. From the young seagull we learn that fear is an illusion of inaction, that the first step must be taken despite trembling, and that encouragement and necessity can awaken courage which scolding never will. From the Dakota pilot we learn presence of mind in crisis, the discipline of following guidance when instruments fail, and trust in help that arrives unexpectedly. Both stories reward calm action over panic: the bird acts on instinct, the pilot acts on faith, and both survive. They also teach gratitude, for the seagull rejoices with his family and the pilot searches the sky to thank his rescuer. Finally, they remind us that every crisis, whether a ledge or a storm, is also a doorway: crossing it turns the timid creature into a flier and the lost pilot into a survivor."
    }
