     "para": "Anne begins her diary by admitting something strange for a girl of thirteen: although she has a loving family, both parents, a sister Margot, about thirty people she could call friends and a crowd of admirers, she has no true friend in whom she can confide everything. Relatives cannot take the place of a friend, and with the best intentions her near ones cannot see into her heart. So she creates a friend on paper, addressing the diary as Kitty and promising to confide in it entirely. Her justification is her most famous line: paper has more patience than people, for it listens without interrupting, judging or showing boredom. Writing, she feels, is the way to bring out all the things that lie buried deep in her heart, and she hopes she will be able to entrust everything to Kitty, since she has never been able to confide in anyone.",
