     "answer": "Anne began the diary because, in the middle of a loving family and about thirty friends, she felt fundamentally alone: she had no single person in whom she could confide everything that lay buried in her heart. Relatives, however dear, cannot replace that one true friend, and a thirteen-year-old girl's unbosomings, she felt, would interest no one. So she decided to create her own friend on paper, naming the diary Kitty, and to confide in it entirely. Her reason is captured in her famous line that paper has more patience than people, for a diary listens without interrupting, judging or growing bored, and writing brings her the relief and satisfaction she cannot find elsewhere."
