     "answer": "1) (a) Mr Keesing kept assigning essays because Anne talked continuously in his maths class, and his first two punishments, 'A Chatterbox' and 'An Incorrigible Chatterbox', failed to silence her, so he used the third title as a joke to mock her as a quacking duck. 2) (b) With her friend Sanne's help, Anne wrote the essay in verse about a mother duck and a father swan with three baby ducklings, who were bitten to death by the father swan because they quacked too much, a witty allegory of the talkative pupil and the punishing teacher. 3) (c) The verse essay was a triumph: the whole class laughed, Keesing took the joke in good humour, read the poem aloud with his own comments and even made further jokes on it in later classes. 4) He stopped assigning Anne extra homework and allowed her to talk in class. 5) The episode reveals Anne's wit, originality and courage, and Keesing's fairness and good humour."
