    {
     "question": "What can a young reader learn from Anne Frank's way of coping with loneliness and difficulties?",
     "marks": 4,
     "type": "value-based",
     "probability": "high",
     "answer": "Anne Frank teaches a young reader three practical virtues. First, express rather than suppress: when no friend could hear her whole heart, she created one on paper, showing that writing, drawing, music or any creative outlet can carry our feelings when people fail us. Second, face authority with intelligence and humour: punished for talking, she neither wept nor revolted but answered with argument and then with a poem, winning respect without breaking respect. Third, keep hope and normality alive under pressure: Anne writes about birthdays, friendships and classroom jokes while the darkest history gathers around her, proving that the human spirit can protect its everyday joys even in danger. Her example tells us that loneliness can be converted into creativity, punishment into performance and fear into hope, and that the habit of honest self-expression, begun early, can leave a gift for the world that outlasts every hardship."
    }
